course: dictionary
lesson: 8917
title: Sideline: the edge of the action
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The sidelines are the lines along the long sides of a sports pitch, and the area just outside them. The word also means watching without taking part, as in «on the sidelines». In «Maddie, the best striker... watched from the sidelines as the team lost 2–1», the context tells you she was outside the game, not playing in it.
explain: Sideline can also be a verb. To sideline someone is to stop them taking part or make them less important. Maddie missed the tournament after her detention, so she was sidelined from the team.
explain: Sideline can also be a noun for a second, smaller job. A sideline selling cakes is a small extra business. Check the words around it: a pitch and players point to the sports meaning, while a job or business points to the extra-work meaning.
explain: In «Jayden scanned the sideline the whole match, played badly and angry», the sideline is where his dad should have been. Jayden is on the pitch, but he feels left out. The context shows the feeling, not just the place.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «On the sidelines» means watching but not taking part.
fact: «Sidelined by injury» means stopped from playing because of an injury.
fact: A sideline is also a second, smaller job or business.
exercise: «Maddie watched from the sidelines as the team lost 2–1.» Where was Maddie? | Running down the wing | *Outside the game, watching | In the goal | Warming up on the pitch
exercise: Mum says Dad's cake baking is a sideline. What does sideline mean here? | A rule about fighting | The edge of a football pitch | *A second, smaller job | A serious injury
exercise: «The coach sidelined the striker for the rest of the season.» What does sidelined mean? | Made captain of the team | *Stopped from taking part | Moved up to the front | Given a medal
test: «Jayden scanned the sideline the whole match.» Where was Jayden looking? | *Along the edge of the pitch, hoping to see his dad | At the scoreboard only | At his muddy boots | Up at the sky
test: Which sentence uses sideline as a verb? | *The coach sidelined the striker for the rest of the season. | The sidelines were freshly painted white. | The sideline was muddy after the rain. | She runs a sideline selling cakes.
exam: Which phrase means watching without taking part? | A sideline job | Sidelined by injury | Along the sideline | *On the sidelines
